(`conventions/outputs.py`), which is the list and which a test holds
the stack's exports to: `kubeconfig`, `talosconfig`, each cloud
node's private IP (`node_private_ips`, the name a Talos call gives
the node, §2), each cloud node's GUA (`node_guas` — with the
private IPv4s, `vip1_private` and the NLB's two addresses, the
`internet` pool's members, which `k8s-base` and `apps` read; rfc-007
§4.4), the worker's GUA (`worker_gua`, derived on every run from the
cluster network's /64 of the site's delegated prefix, which the run
reads off the gateway, §4, and so
always present — the worker's own pool in `k8s-base` and a
`SiteForward` port's Service in `apps` read it; rfc-008 §4.1), each
cloud node's volumes by name to the OCID OCI generated for each
(`volume_ids` — the node's boot volume under `boot`, and each row of
`conventions.NODE_VOLUMES` under its own name on the node it is
attached to, §1, so no row may be named `boot`; OCI's block-volume
metrics name a volume by its OCID alone, and the map is what turns
one back into a node and a name; a reader takes it through
`kluster.lib.stack_volumes`), both
of the NLB's public addresses (IPv4 and IPv6 — the cluster anchor in
`dns` carries an A and an AAAA), the
dedicated-VIP addresses (reserved public + secondary private), the
backup bucket's name and S3 endpoint, the backup keys (one application
key per scope, `backup_keys`), and each CI overlay member's join
credential (`ci_zerotier_identity_physical`,
`ci_zerotier_identity_dns`).

## 1. OCI (pulumi-oci)

-   **VCN**: dual-stack (IPv4 + the assigned /56 GUA), one public
    subnet, internet gateway, and a **service gateway** (2026-08-24 —
    node ↔ Object Storage/OCIR traffic rides the in-region $0 path
    instead of the IGW). **The subnet narrows no port**: it carries one
    security list of this stack's own. It admits every IPv4 protocol
    from `0.0.0.0/0`; over IPv6 it admits TCP and UDP from `::/0`, the
    four ICMPv6 error types and echo request and reply from `::/0`, and
    every ICMPv6 type from `fe80::/10` and from the subnet's own IPv6
    block. ICMPv6 is narrowed because Talos admits it through one rate
    allowance that neighbor discovery shares, so only the list can bound
    a flood (cluster/security-audit.md M3, which also records what that
    leaves open). The list lets every protocol out to both families, and
    every rule is stateless. A subnet naming no list would carry the
    VCN's default one, whose rules are OCI's choice rather than this
    program's, so the list is declared and is the subnet's only one.
    Stateless because a rule that decides by protocol, type and source
    has nothing to decide by a connection's state, while tracking it
    costs a table on each VNIC that drops new connections when it fills.
    The list applies to the NLB's VNIC too, which answers only on its
    listeners. **The filter on ports is the node's**: the Talos ingress
    firewall decides every port in the host network namespace (§2), and
    what Cilium's datapath answers ahead of it is the declared raw
    TCP/UDP Services, while no Service allocates a `NodePort`
    (cluster-infra.md §2).
-   **Image**: no official Talos OCI image — an `image_factory_schematic`
    (talos provider) pins the schematic (platform `oracle`, arm64, no
    extensions initially), and a custom-image import brings the
    factory-built image into OCI. OCI imports only from its own Object
    Storage, so the import takes the path Talos's Oracle guide gives:
    the factory's `oracle-arm64.qcow2` is fetched onto the machine
    running the apply, packaged with an `image_metadata.json` (UEFI
    firmware, `VM.Standard.A1.Flex` among the compatible shapes) into
    one `tar` archive compressed with `gzip`, uploaded to a
